// Edexcel (9CH0) flashcards, keyed by spec topic number. HSJ-written.
export const EDEXCEL_SETS = {
  "edx_1": { title: "Atomic structure and the periodic table", cards: [
    {q:"State the relative mass and charge of a proton, neutron and electron.", a:"Proton: mass 1, charge +1. Neutron: mass 1, charge 0. Electron: mass 1/1840 (negligible), charge −1."},
    {q:"Define atomic number and mass number.", a:"Atomic (proton) number Z = number of protons. Mass (nucleon) number A = protons + neutrons."},
    {q:"Define isotopes.", a:"Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons (different mass numbers). Same chemical properties, slightly different physical properties."},
    {q:"Define relative atomic mass.", a:"The weighted mean mass of an atom of an element compared with 1/12 of the mass of an atom of carbon-12."},
    {q:"Define relative isotopic mass.", a:"The mass of an atom of an isotope compared with 1/12 of the mass of an atom of carbon-12."},
    {q:"How is relative atomic mass calculated from a mass spectrum?", a:"Ar = Σ(isotopic mass × % abundance) ÷ 100. Example: chlorine (75% ³⁵Cl, 25% ³⁷Cl) gives Ar = 35.5."},
    {q:"Outline the stages in a time-of-flight mass spectrometer.", a:"Ionisation (electron impact or electrospray), acceleration by an electric field so ions have the same kinetic energy, drift through the flight tube (lighter ions travel faster), detection as ions hit the detector and a current is measured."},
    {q:"How does a mass spectrum of Cl₂ show the isotopes?", a:"Molecular ion peaks at m/z 70 (³⁵Cl³⁵Cl), 72 (³⁵Cl³⁷Cl) and 74 (³⁷Cl³⁷Cl) in the ratio 9 : 6 : 1."},
    {q:"Define first ionisation energy.", a:"The energy required to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms to form one mole of gaseous 1+ ions. X(g) → X⁺(g) + e⁻."},
    {q:"Write the equation for the second ionisation energy of magnesium.", a:"Mg⁺(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + e⁻"},
    {q:"State three factors that affect ionisation energy.", a:"Nuclear charge (more protons, stronger attraction), atomic radius (further from nucleus, weaker attraction), shielding by inner electron shells (more shielding, weaker attraction)."},
    {q:"Why is there a large jump between the second and third ionisation energies of magnesium?", a:"The third electron is removed from the second shell (n = 2), which is closer to the nucleus and less shielded than the outer shell, so much more energy is needed."},
    {q:"Why do successive ionisation energies always increase?", a:"Each electron is removed from an increasingly positive ion, so the remaining electrons are held more tightly. There is also less electron–electron repulsion."},
    {q:"Why does first ionisation energy decrease down Group 2?", a:"Atomic radius increases and shielding increases; these outweigh the increased nuclear charge, so the outer electron is lost more easily."},
    {q:"Why does first ionisation energy generally increase across Period 3?", a:"Nuclear charge increases while the electrons enter the same shell with similar shielding, so the outer electrons are attracted more strongly."},
    {q:"Why is the first ionisation energy of aluminium lower than that of magnesium?", a:"Al's outer electron is in a 3p orbital, higher in energy and slightly more shielded than Mg's 3s electron, so it is easier to remove."},
    {q:"Why is the first ionisation energy of sulfur lower than that of phosphorus?", a:"In sulfur the electron is removed from a 3p orbital containing a pair of electrons. The repulsion between the paired electrons makes it easier to remove."},
    {q:"How many orbitals and electrons are in the s, p and d subshells?", a:"s: 1 orbital, 2 electrons. p: 3 orbitals, 6 electrons. d: 5 orbitals, 10 electrons."},
    {q:"Write the electron configuration of iron and of Fe³⁺.", a:"Fe: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ 4s². Fe³⁺: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ (4s electrons lost first, then one 3d)."},
    {q:"Describe the shapes of s and p orbitals.", a:"s orbitals are spherical. p orbitals are dumbbell shaped, arranged at right angles along the x, y and z axes."},
    {q:"What is periodicity?", a:"A repeating pattern in physical or chemical properties across each period of the periodic table, such as atomic radius, ionisation energy and melting point."},
    {q:"Explain the trend in melting points across Period 3.", a:"Na, Mg, Al: metallic bonding gets stronger (more delocalised electrons, higher charge) so melting point rises. Si: giant covalent, very high. P₄, S₈, Cl₂, Ar: simple molecular with weak London forces, low; S₈ highest of these as it is the largest molecule."},
    {q:"Why does atomic radius decrease across a period?", a:"Nuclear charge increases while electrons are added to the same shell with similar shielding, so the electrons are pulled closer to the nucleus."},
    {q:"Which block of the periodic table does an element belong to?", a:"The block is named after the subshell that holds its highest-energy electron: s block (Groups 1, 2), p block (Groups 3 to 0), d block (transition metals)."},
  ]},
  "edx_2": { title: "Bonding and structure", cards: [
    {q:"Define ionic bonding.", a:"The strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions in a giant ionic lattice."},
    {q:"How does ionic radius change down a group and with charge?", a:"Radius increases down a group (more shells). For isoelectronic ions (e.g. N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺) radius decreases as nuclear charge increases."},
    {q:"State the evidence for the existence of ions.", a:"Electron density maps show separate spheres with zero density between them; electrolysis of coloured ionic compounds (e.g. copper(II) chromate) shows ions migrating to opposite electrodes."},
    {q:"Explain the physical properties of ionic compounds.", a:"High melting points (strong attraction between many ions); conduct electricity only when molten or dissolved (ions free to move); often soluble in water (polar water molecules attract ions); brittle."},
    {q:"Define covalent bonding.", a:"The strong electrostatic attraction between two nuclei and the shared pair of electrons between them."},
    {q:"What is a dative covalent bond? Give an example.", a:"A covalent bond where both electrons in the shared pair come from the same atom, e.g. NH₄⁺ (N donates to H⁺), Al₂Cl₆, H₃O⁺."},
    {q:"How do bond length and bond strength relate?", a:"Shorter bonds are stronger. Multiple bonds are shorter and stronger than single bonds between the same atoms (C≡C < C=C < C–C in length)."},
    {q:"Describe metallic bonding.", a:"The strong electrostatic attraction between a regular lattice of positive metal ions and the sea of delocalised electrons."},
    {q:"Explain why metals conduct electricity and are malleable.", a:"Delocalised electrons are free to move and carry charge. Layers of ions can slide over each other while the delocalised electrons keep the attraction."},
    {q:"State electron-pair repulsion theory.", a:"Electron pairs around a central atom repel and arrange themselves as far apart as possible. Lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs, so lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair > bond pair–bond pair."},
    {q:"Give the shapes and bond angles for 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 bonding pairs with no lone pairs.", a:"Linear 180°; trigonal planar 120°; tetrahedral 109.5°; trigonal bipyramidal 90° and 120°; octahedral 90°."},
    {q:"What are the shapes of NH₃ and H₂O?", a:"NH₃: trigonal pyramidal, 107°. H₂O: non-linear (V-shaped), 104.5°. Each lone pair reduces the angle by about 2.5°."},
    {q:"Define electronegativity.", a:"The ability of an atom to attract the bonding electrons (electron pair) in a covalent bond."},
    {q:"How does electronegativity change across the periodic table?", a:"Increases across a period (more nuclear charge, smaller radius) and decreases down a group (more shielding, larger radius). Fluorine is the most electronegative."},
    {q:"When is a molecule polar?", a:"When it contains polar bonds and the dipoles do not cancel because the molecule is not symmetrical, e.g. HCl, H₂O, NH₃, CHCl₃. CO₂ and CCl₄ are non-polar."},
    {q:"Describe London forces (instantaneous dipole–induced dipole).", a:"Random electron movement creates a temporary dipole that induces a dipole in a neighbouring molecule, giving a weak attraction. Stronger with more electrons and more points of contact (less branching)."},
    {q:"Describe permanent dipole–permanent dipole interactions.", a:"Attraction between the δ+ end of one polar molecule and the δ− end of another. Stronger than London forces for molecules of similar size."},
    {q:"What is needed for hydrogen bonding?", a:"A hydrogen atom bonded to N, O or F (very electronegative, δ+ H) and a lone pair on an N, O or F atom in another molecule."},
    {q:"Explain the anomalous properties of water.", a:"Hydrogen bonding gives a high boiling point, high surface tension and a solid (ice) less dense than the liquid because the hydrogen bonds hold molecules in an open lattice."},
    {q:"Why do boiling points of alkanes increase with chain length?", a:"More electrons and larger surface area give stronger London forces, so more energy is needed to separate the molecules."},
    {q:"Why do the boiling points of the hydrogen halides increase from HCl to HI but HF is anomalous?", a:"HCl to HI: more electrons, stronger London forces. HF is higher because it forms hydrogen bonds."},
    {q:"Describe the structure and properties of diamond and graphite.", a:"Diamond: each C bonded to 4 others, tetrahedral, giant covalent, very hard, insulator. Graphite: layers of hexagons, each C bonded to 3, delocalised electrons conduct, layers slide (soft)."},
    {q:"Why do alcohols dissolve in water but alkanes do not?", a:"Alcohols form hydrogen bonds with water through the O–H group. Alkanes cannot hydrogen bond, so they only have weak London forces and are insoluble."},
    {q:"What determines whether an ionic compound dissolves in water?", a:"The energy released when polar water molecules hydrate the ions must be enough to overcome the lattice energy."},
  ]},
  "edx_3": { title: "Redox I", cards: [
    {q:"Define oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons.", a:"Oxidation Is Loss of electrons, Reduction Is Gain (OIL RIG). Oxidation increases oxidation number; reduction decreases it."},
    {q:"State the rules for assigning oxidation numbers.", a:"Uncombined element 0. Simple ion = its charge. O usually −2 (−1 in peroxides). H usually +1 (−1 in metal hydrides). F always −1. Sum = overall charge of species."},
    {q:"What is the oxidation number of Cl in ClO₃⁻?", a:"+5. Three oxygens = −6; overall charge −1, so Cl = +5."},
    {q:"What is the oxidation number of N in NH₄⁺?", a:"−3. Four hydrogens = +4; overall +1, so N = −3."},
    {q:"How do you name compounds using Roman numerals?", a:"The oxidation number of the element is given in Roman numerals after the name, e.g. iron(III) chloride FeCl₃, sodium chlorate(V) NaClO₃."},
    {q:"Define an oxidising agent.", a:"A species that accepts electrons from another species and is itself reduced, e.g. Cl₂, MnO₄⁻, Cr₂O₇²⁻."},
    {q:"Define a reducing agent.", a:"A species that donates electrons to another species and is itself oxidised, e.g. metals, I⁻, Fe²⁺."},
    {q:"Write the half-equations for the reaction of magnesium with chlorine.", a:"Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ (oxidation); Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻ (reduction)."},
    {q:"How do you combine half-equations?", a:"Balance the electrons so that electrons lost = electrons gained (multiply the half-equations), then add and cancel the electrons."},
    {q:"Write the ionic equation for zinc reacting with copper(II) ions.", a:"Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu. Zinc is oxidised (0 to +2), copper is reduced (+2 to 0)."},
    {q:"Define disproportionation.", a:"A reaction in which the same element is simultaneously oxidised and reduced."},
    {q:"Give an example of disproportionation.", a:"Cl₂ + H₂O → HCl + HClO: chlorine goes from 0 to −1 (reduced) and 0 to +1 (oxidised). Also Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O."},
    {q:"How do you balance a half-equation in acidic solution?", a:"Balance atoms other than O and H; add H₂O to balance O; add H⁺ to balance H; add electrons to balance charge."},
    {q:"Write the half-equation for MnO₄⁻ being reduced to Mn²⁺ in acid.", a:"MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O"},
    {q:"Write the half-equation for Cr₂O₇²⁻ being reduced to Cr³⁺ in acid.", a:"Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O"},
    {q:"Write the overall equation for MnO₄⁻ oxidising Fe²⁺.", a:"MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺"},
    {q:"In the reaction 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂, which species is the oxidising agent?", a:"Fe³⁺ is the oxidising agent (it gains electrons, +3 to +2). I⁻ is the reducing agent (oxidised from −1 to 0)."},
    {q:"What is the oxidation number of sulfur in H₂SO₄ and in S₂O₃²⁻?", a:"H₂SO₄: +6. S₂O₃²⁻: +2 (average)."},
    {q:"Why do metals react with acids in redox reactions?", a:"The metal is oxidised to its positive ion (loses electrons) and H⁺ is reduced to H₂ (gains electrons): Mg + 2H⁺ → Mg²⁺ + H₂."},
    {q:"How do you identify a redox reaction from an equation?", a:"Assign oxidation numbers to every element; if any element's oxidation number changes it is a redox reaction."},
    {q:"In 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂, which element disproportionates?", a:"Oxygen: from −1 in H₂O₂ to −2 in water (reduced) and 0 in O₂ (oxidised)."},
    {q:"Why is the oxidation number of oxygen in OF₂ +2?", a:"Fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen and is always −1, so two fluorines (−2) give oxygen +2."},
    {q:"What is the change in oxidation number of chlorine when ClO⁻ forms Cl⁻?", a:"+1 to −1, a decrease of 2: chlorine is reduced and gains 2 electrons."},
    {q:"Write the half-equation for the oxidation of hydrogen peroxide to oxygen.", a:"H₂O₂ → O₂ + 2H⁺ + 2e⁻"},
  ]},
  "edx_4": { title: "Inorganic chemistry and the periodic table", cards: [
    {q:"Describe the reaction of Group 2 metals with oxygen.", a:"They burn to form the metal oxide: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (bright white flame). Reactivity increases down the group."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of Group 2 metals with water.", a:"M + 2H₂O → M(OH)₂ + H₂. Mg reacts very slowly with cold water (faster with steam giving MgO), Ca fizzes steadily, Ba reacts vigorously."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of Group 2 metals with dilute acids.", a:"M + 2HCl → MCl₂ + H₂. Vigour increases down the group as ionisation energy falls."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of Group 2 oxides with water.", a:"MO + H₂O → M(OH)₂. MgO reacts slightly (pH about 9); BaO reacts readily giving a strongly alkaline solution (pH about 13)."},
    {q:"How do Group 2 oxides and hydroxides react with acids?", a:"They act as bases: MgO + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂O; Ca(OH)₂ + 2HNO₃ → Ca(NO₃)₂ + 2H₂O."},
    {q:"State the trend in solubility of Group 2 hydroxides and sulfates.", a:"Hydroxides become more soluble down the group (Mg(OH)₂ sparingly soluble, Ba(OH)₂ soluble). Sulfates become less soluble (MgSO₄ soluble, BaSO₄ insoluble)."},
    {q:"Explain the trend in thermal stability of Group 2 carbonates and nitrates.", a:"Stability increases down the group. Smaller cations with higher charge density polarise (distort) the anion more, weakening the C–O or N–O bonds so decomposition occurs at a lower temperature."},
    {q:"Write the equations for the thermal decomposition of Group 2 carbonates and nitrates.", a:"MCO₃ → MO + CO₂. 2M(NO₃)₂ → 2MO + 4NO₂ + O₂ (brown gas, oxygen relights a splint)."},
    {q:"How do Group 1 nitrates decompose?", a:"Group 1 nitrates (except lithium) decompose to the nitrite: 2NaNO₃ → 2NaNO₂ + O₂. Lithium nitrate decomposes like Group 2: 4LiNO₃ → 2Li₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂."},
    {q:"How does flame testing work and what colours are seen?", a:"Electrons are excited by the heat and emit light of a characteristic frequency when they drop back. Li red, Na yellow, K lilac, Ca brick red, Sr crimson, Ba apple green."},
    {q:"State the trend in electronegativity and boiling point of the halogens.", a:"Electronegativity decreases down the group (larger atoms, more shielding). Boiling point increases down the group (more electrons, stronger London forces)."},
    {q:"State the colours and states of the halogens at room temperature.", a:"F₂ pale yellow gas, Cl₂ green gas, Br₂ red-brown liquid, I₂ grey-black solid (purple vapour). In cyclohexane: Cl₂ colourless, Br₂ orange, I₂ violet."},
    {q:"Explain the trend in oxidising ability of the halogens.", a:"Decreases down the group. Larger atoms have more shielding and a greater distance to the nucleus, so they attract an extra electron less strongly."},
    {q:"What is observed when chlorine is added to potassium bromide and potassium iodide solutions?", a:"KBr: solution turns orange (Br₂ formed). KI: solution turns brown (I₂ formed). Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂."},
    {q:"Explain the trend in reducing ability of the halide ions.", a:"Increases down the group. Larger ions lose their outer electron more easily because it is further from the nucleus and more shielded."},
    {q:"Describe the reactions of the halide ions with concentrated sulfuric acid.", a:"Cl⁻: HCl fumes only (not oxidised). Br⁻: HBr then Br₂ (orange vapour) and SO₂. I⁻: HI then I₂ (purple vapour/black solid), SO₂, S (yellow) and H₂S (bad egg smell)."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of chlorine with water, and in sunlight.", a:"Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO (disproportionation; used in water treatment). In sunlight: 2Cl₂ + 2H₂O → 4HCl + O₂."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of chlorine with cold dilute sodium hydroxide.", a:"Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O. Disproportionation; NaClO is bleach."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of chlorine with hot concentrated sodium hydroxide.", a:"3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O. Chlorine goes to −1 and +5."},
    {q:"How do you test for halide ions?", a:"Add dilute nitric acid (to remove carbonate), then silver nitrate. AgCl white (dissolves in dilute NH₃), AgBr cream (dissolves in concentrated NH₃), AgI yellow (insoluble in NH₃)."},
    {q:"Describe the reactions of hydrogen halides with ammonia and with water.", a:"HX + NH₃ → NH₄X (white smoke of the ammonium halide). HX dissolves in water to form a strong acid: HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻."},
    {q:"How do you test for sulfate ions?", a:"Add dilute HCl then barium chloride solution: a white precipitate of BaSO₄ forms."},
    {q:"How do you test for carbonate ions?", a:"Add dilute acid: effervescence; the gas turns limewater milky (CO₂)."},
    {q:"How do you test for ammonium ions?", a:"Add sodium hydroxide and warm: ammonia gas is released, which turns damp red litmus paper blue."},
  ]},
  "edx_5": { title: "Formulae, equations and amounts of substance", cards: [
    {q:"Define a mole and the Avogadro constant.", a:"A mole is the amount of substance containing 6.02 × 10²³ particles (the Avogadro constant, L), the same number as atoms in 12 g of carbon-12."},
    {q:"Give the three key mole equations.", a:"n = mass / molar mass; n = concentration × volume (dm³); n = volume (dm³) / 24 (gases at RTP)."},
    {q:"State the ideal gas equation and its units.", a:"pV = nRT. p in Pa, V in m³, n in mol, R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, T in K. Convert cm³ to m³ by × 10⁻⁶, kPa to Pa by × 10³, °C to K by + 273."},
    {q:"Define empirical formula and molecular formula.", a:"Empirical: simplest whole number ratio of atoms of each element. Molecular: actual number of atoms of each element in a molecule."},
    {q:"How do you find an empirical formula from percentage composition?", a:"Divide each % by the Ar to get moles, divide all by the smallest, then multiply to get whole numbers."},
    {q:"A compound is 40.0% C, 6.7% H, 53.3% O. What is its empirical formula?", a:"C: 40/12 = 3.33; H: 6.7/1 = 6.7; O: 53.3/16 = 3.33. Ratio 1 : 2 : 1 so CH₂O."},
    {q:"How do you determine the formula of a hydrated salt?", a:"Heat to constant mass; mass lost = water. n(anhydrous salt) and n(water) from masses; ratio gives x in MX·xH₂O."},
    {q:"Define the term limiting reagent.", a:"The reactant that is completely used up first and so determines the maximum amount of product. Calculate moles of each and compare with the equation ratio."},
    {q:"Define percentage yield.", a:"% yield = (actual moles of product / theoretical moles of product) × 100."},
    {q:"Define atom economy.", a:"% atom economy = (Mr of desired product / sum of Mr of all products) × 100, using the balanced equation. Addition reactions are 100%."},
    {q:"Why is high atom economy important?", a:"Less waste, more sustainable, more efficient use of raw materials, lower costs. Yield can be improved by changing conditions, but atom economy is fixed by the equation."},
    {q:"What is a standard solution and how is it made?", a:"A solution of known concentration. Weigh the solid accurately, dissolve in a little distilled water in a beaker, transfer with washings to a volumetric flask, fill to the line, invert to mix."},
    {q:"Describe how to carry out an acid–base titration.", a:"Pipette 25.0 cm³ of one solution into a conical flask, add indicator, titrate from a burette with swirling until the colour change, repeat until concordant results (within 0.10 cm³) and take the mean titre."},
    {q:"In a titration, 25.0 cm³ of NaOH needed 22.5 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ HCl. What is the NaOH concentration?", a:"n(HCl) = 0.100 × 0.0225 = 2.25 × 10⁻³ mol = n(NaOH). c = 2.25 × 10⁻³ / 0.0250 = 0.0900 mol dm⁻³."},
    {q:"How do you calculate percentage uncertainty for a measurement?", a:"% uncertainty = (uncertainty / measured value) × 100. For a burette (±0.05 cm³ per reading, two readings) the titre uncertainty is ±0.10 cm³."},
    {q:"How can percentage uncertainty be reduced?", a:"Use larger quantities (bigger mass, larger titre) or more precise equipment, so the fixed uncertainty is a smaller fraction of the reading."},
    {q:"State Avogadro's law.", a:"Equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules, so volume ratios equal mole ratios in gas reactions."},
    {q:"What volume of CO₂ at RTP forms when 5.0 g of CaCO₃ decomposes?", a:"n(CaCO₃) = 5.0/100 = 0.050 mol = n(CO₂). V = 0.050 × 24 = 1.2 dm³."},
    {q:"How do you convert between mol dm⁻³ and g dm⁻³?", a:"g dm⁻³ = mol dm⁻³ × molar mass."},
    {q:"What are concordant titres?", a:"Titres that agree to within 0.10 cm³ of each other. Only concordant titres are used to calculate the mean."},
    {q:"How do you write an ionic equation?", a:"Write the full equation, split soluble ionic compounds into ions, cancel spectator ions that appear unchanged on both sides."},
    {q:"Write the ionic equation for the precipitation of barium sulfate.", a:"Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s)"},
    {q:"How many molecules are in 0.25 mol of water?", a:"0.25 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.5 × 10²³ molecules."},
    {q:"How many atoms are in 0.25 mol of water?", a:"Each molecule has 3 atoms: 3 × 0.25 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 4.5 × 10²³ atoms."},
  ]},
  "edx_6": { title: "Organic chemistry I", cards: [
    {q:"Define homologous series.", a:"A series of compounds with the same functional group and general formula, where each member differs from the next by CH₂, and which show a gradual trend in physical properties and similar chemical reactions."},
    {q:"Define functional group.", a:"The atom or group of atoms responsible for the characteristic chemical reactions of a compound, e.g. C=C, –OH, –Br."},
    {q:"Define structural isomers and name the three types.", a:"Same molecular formula, different structural formula. Chain isomers (different carbon skeleton), position isomers (functional group in a different position), functional group isomers (different functional group)."},
    {q:"Define stereoisomers and E/Z isomerism.", a:"Same structural formula, different arrangement in space. E/Z isomerism occurs in alkenes because the C=C cannot rotate and each carbon has two different groups. E: highest priority groups on opposite sides; Z: same side."},
    {q:"What is the Cahn–Ingold–Prelog priority rule?", a:"On each carbon of the C=C, the group whose first atom has the higher atomic number has priority. If the same, move to the next atoms along the chain."},
    {q:"Describe the reactions of alkanes with oxygen.", a:"Complete: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ + excess O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O. Incomplete (limited oxygen): CO (toxic, binds to haemoglobin) and C (soot) also form."},
    {q:"What pollutants form when fuels burn in engines, and how are they removed?", a:"CO, NOₓ (N₂ + O₂ at high temperature), unburnt hydrocarbons, SO₂, particulates. Catalytic converters: 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂. Flue gas desulfurisation uses CaO or CaCO₃."},
    {q:"Describe the free radical substitution of methane with chlorine.", a:"Initiation: Cl₂ → 2Cl• (UV). Propagation: CH₄ + Cl• → •CH₃ + HCl; •CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•. Termination: two radicals combine, e.g. •CH₃ + Cl• → CH₃Cl."},
    {q:"Why is free radical substitution not useful for making a single product?", a:"Further substitution gives a mixture (CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃, CCl₄) and termination steps give longer-chain by-products such as ethane."},
    {q:"Describe the bonding in a C=C double bond.", a:"A σ bond from head-on overlap of orbitals and a π bond from sideways overlap of p orbitals above and below the plane. The π bond is weaker and is the site of electrophilic attack."},
    {q:"Describe the electrophilic addition of bromine to ethene.", a:"The electron-rich C=C induces a dipole in Br₂. Br δ+ is attacked, the C=C electrons form a C–Br bond and Br–Br breaks heterolytically giving Br⁻. The carbocation is attacked by Br⁻ to give 1,2-dibromoethane."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of propene with HBr and explain the major product.", a:"Electrophilic addition. H⁺ adds first; the secondary carbocation (CH₃CH⁺CH₃) is more stable than the primary because alkyl groups push electrons towards the positive carbon, so 2-bromopropane is the major product."},
    {q:"How is an alkene converted to an alcohol industrially?", a:"Steam hydration: ethene + H₂O(g) at 300 °C, 60 to 70 atm, phosphoric acid catalyst → ethanol."},
    {q:"What are the products of alkenes with potassium manganate(VII)?", a:"Acidified KMnO₄ (purple) is decolourised and a diol is formed: ethene → ethane-1,2-diol. Used as a test for C=C."},
    {q:"Describe hydrogenation of alkenes.", a:"Alkene + H₂ with a nickel catalyst at about 150 °C gives the alkane. Used to harden vegetable oils to make margarine."},
    {q:"Describe addition polymerisation and draw the repeat unit of poly(propene).", a:"Many alkene monomers join when the C=C opens, no other product. Poly(propene) repeat unit: –CH₂–CH(CH₃)– with brackets and n."},
    {q:"Give three ways of dealing with waste polymers.", a:"Recycling (sort, melt, remould), incineration with energy recovery (releases CO₂ and possibly HCl from PVC), feedstock recycling (crack into monomers), landfill (last resort). Biodegradable polymers reduce waste."},
    {q:"Describe the nucleophilic substitution of a haloalkane with aqueous hydroxide.", a:"OH⁻ lone pair attacks the δ+ carbon, the C–X bond breaks heterolytically, giving an alcohol and a halide ion. Conditions: warm with aqueous NaOH (or KOH)."},
    {q:"Why do iodoalkanes hydrolyse faster than chloroalkanes?", a:"The C–I bond is longer and weaker (lower bond enthalpy) than C–Cl, so it breaks more easily. Bond strength matters more than bond polarity."},
    {q:"How is the rate of hydrolysis of haloalkanes compared experimentally?", a:"Warm each haloalkane with ethanol (solvent) and aqueous silver nitrate. Time how long the precipitate takes: AgI (yellow) fastest, AgCl (white) slowest."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of a haloalkane with ammonia.", a:"Nucleophilic substitution with excess ethanolic ammonia, heated under pressure: C₂H₅Br + 2NH₃ → C₂H₅NH₂ + NH₄Br. Excess NH₃ reduces further substitution."},
    {q:"Describe the elimination reaction of haloalkanes.", a:"Heat with ethanolic potassium hydroxide: the OH⁻ acts as a base, removing an H from the carbon next to the C–X, forming an alkene and water plus the halide ion."},
    {q:"Describe the oxidation of primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols.", a:"Primary → aldehyde (distil) → carboxylic acid (reflux, excess oxidant). Secondary → ketone. Tertiary: not oxidised. Oxidant: acidified potassium dichromate(VI), orange to green."},
    {q:"How are haloalkanes made from alcohols?", a:"With PCl₅ (gives HCl fumes, a test for OH), with KBr and 50% H₂SO₄ (forms HBr in situ), or with red phosphorus and iodine (forms PI₃)."},
  ]},
  "edx_7": { title: "Modern analytical techniques I", cards: [
    {q:"What does the molecular ion peak in a mass spectrum tell you?", a:"The peak with the highest m/z (ignoring the M+1 peak) gives the relative molecular mass of the compound."},
    {q:"What is the M+1 peak?", a:"A small peak one unit above the molecular ion, caused by molecules containing a ¹³C atom (1.1% natural abundance)."},
    {q:"How does fragmentation occur in a mass spectrometer?", a:"The molecular ion is unstable and breaks into a positive fragment ion (detected) and a radical (not detected): M⁺ → X⁺ + Y•."},
    {q:"Which fragment ions do m/z 15, 29, 43 and 57 suggest?", a:"15: CH₃⁺. 29: C₂H₅⁺ or CHO⁺. 43: C₃H₇⁺ or CH₃CO⁺. 57: C₄H₉⁺."},
    {q:"How can mass spectra distinguish propanal from propanone?", a:"Both have M = 58. Propanone gives a strong peak at m/z 43 (CH₃CO⁺) and 15 (CH₃⁺). Propanal gives m/z 29 (CHO⁺ and C₂H₅⁺) and 57 (loss of H)."},
    {q:"Why are halogenated compounds recognisable in mass spectra?", a:"Chlorine gives two molecular ion peaks (M and M+2) in a 3 : 1 ratio; bromine gives M and M+2 in a 1 : 1 ratio."},
    {q:"What does infrared spectroscopy detect?", a:"Covalent bonds absorb IR radiation at characteristic frequencies and vibrate (stretch or bend) more. The absorptions identify functional groups."},
    {q:"Which bonds absorb in these regions: 1650 to 1750, 2500 to 3300 broad, 3200 to 3550 broad, 3300 to 3500?", a:"C=O (carbonyl); O–H in a carboxylic acid; O–H in an alcohol; N–H in an amine."},
    {q:"What is the fingerprint region and how is it used?", a:"Below 1500 cm⁻¹. The complex pattern is unique to each compound and can be matched with a database spectrum to identify it."},
    {q:"How would you tell an alcohol from a carboxylic acid using IR?", a:"Both have O–H, but the acid's O–H is very broad at 2500 to 3300 cm⁻¹ and it also has a C=O absorption at about 1700 cm⁻¹."},
    {q:"How would you distinguish an aldehyde from an alcohol using IR?", a:"The aldehyde shows a strong C=O absorption at about 1700 cm⁻¹ and no O–H; the alcohol shows a broad O–H at 3200 to 3550 cm⁻¹ and no C=O."},
    {q:"How does IR spectroscopy relate to global warming?", a:"C=O, O–H and C–H bonds in CO₂, H₂O and CH₄ absorb IR radiation emitted by the Earth, so these greenhouse gases trap heat."},
    {q:"Give two uses of IR spectroscopy.", a:"Roadside breathalysers (measuring the C–H absorption of ethanol) and monitoring air pollution (e.g. CO levels)."},
    {q:"How is mass spectrometry used outside the laboratory?", a:"Drug testing in sport, carbon-14 dating, identifying compounds in space probes, pharmacological studies, forensic analysis."},
    {q:"What is meant by m/z?", a:"Mass-to-charge ratio. Most ions have a charge of 1+, so m/z equals the mass of the ion."},
    {q:"Suggest the fragmentation that gives m/z 31 in the spectrum of ethanol.", a:"Loss of CH₃• from CH₃CH₂OH⁺ leaves CH₂OH⁺, m/z 31."},
    {q:"In the mass spectrum of butane, what is the peak at m/z 43?", a:"C₃H₇⁺, formed when the molecular ion C₄H₁₀⁺ (58) loses a methyl radical •CH₃."},
    {q:"Why is the molecular ion peak sometimes very small?", a:"The molecular ion is unstable and almost all of it fragments before reaching the detector."},
    {q:"How do you write an equation for the formation of a fragment ion?", a:"The molecular ion splits into a cation and a radical: [CH₃CH₂CH₂OH]⁺ → [CH₃CH₂]⁺ + •CH₂OH."},
    {q:"Explain why a C=O bond absorbs IR but O₂ and N₂ do not.", a:"Absorption needs a change in dipole during the vibration. C=O is polar; the symmetrical O=O and N≡N bonds have no dipole change."},
    {q:"Which IR absorption shows a C–H bond?", a:"About 2850 to 3100 cm⁻¹, present in almost all organic compounds so not very diagnostic."},
    {q:"How would IR show that an oxidation of propan-1-ol to propanoic acid is complete?", a:"The alcohol O–H band (3200 to 3550) disappears and a C=O band (about 1700) plus a very broad acid O–H (2500 to 3300) appear."},
    {q:"What information do mass spectrometry and IR together give about an unknown compound?", a:"MS gives molecular mass and fragments (structure clues); IR identifies the functional groups present. Together they often identify a simple compound."},
    {q:"Why are the absorption wavenumbers of C=O slightly different in aldehydes, ketones, acids and esters?", a:"Neighbouring atoms alter the bond strength slightly (electron withdrawal or donation), shifting the frequency. Data tables give ranges for each type."},
  ]},
  "edx_8": { title: "Energetics I", cards: [
    {q:"Define enthalpy change.", a:"The heat energy change measured at constant pressure, ΔH. Exothermic: ΔH negative (heat released). Endothermic: ΔH positive (heat absorbed)."},
    {q:"State standard conditions.", a:"100 kPa (1 bar) pressure, a stated temperature (usually 298 K), all substances in their standard states and solutions at 1 mol dm⁻³."},
    {q:"Define standard enthalpy change of reaction.", a:"The enthalpy change when the amounts of reactants shown in the equation react under standard conditions, with everything in its standard state."},
    {q:"Define standard enthalpy change of formation.", a:"The enthalpy change when one mole of a compound is formed from its elements under standard conditions, all in their standard states. ΔfH of an element = 0."},
    {q:"Define standard enthalpy change of combustion.", a:"The enthalpy change when one mole of a substance is burned completely in oxygen under standard conditions, all in their standard states."},
    {q:"Define standard enthalpy change of neutralisation.", a:"The enthalpy change when one mole of water is formed by the reaction of an acid with a base under standard conditions. About −57 kJ mol⁻¹ for strong acid and strong base."},
    {q:"Define standard enthalpy change of atomisation.", a:"The enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous atoms is formed from the element in its standard state, e.g. ½Cl₂(g) → Cl(g)."},
    {q:"State the equation for heat energy transferred in calorimetry.", a:"q = m c ΔT, where m is the mass of water or solution (g), c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ and ΔT is the temperature change. ΔH = −q / n (in kJ mol⁻¹)."},
    {q:"50 cm³ of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ HCl reacts with 50 cm³ of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ NaOH and the temperature rises 6.5 °C. Calculate ΔH neutralisation.", a:"q = 100 × 4.18 × 6.5 = 2717 J. n(water) = 0.050 mol. ΔH = −2717 / 0.050 = −54 300 J mol⁻¹ = −54.3 kJ mol⁻¹."},
    {q:"Why are experimental enthalpies of combustion less exothermic than data-book values?", a:"Heat loss to the surroundings, incomplete combustion, evaporation of fuel, and the heat capacity of the apparatus is ignored."},
    {q:"How can heat loss be corrected for in a calorimetry experiment?", a:"Plot temperature against time, extrapolate the cooling line back to the time of mixing and read the corrected maximum temperature."},
    {q:"State Hess's law.", a:"The total enthalpy change of a reaction is independent of the route taken, provided the initial and final conditions are the same."},
    {q:"How is ΔH of reaction calculated from enthalpies of formation?", a:"ΔrH = ΣΔfH(products) − ΣΔfH(reactants)."},
    {q:"How is ΔH of reaction calculated from enthalpies of combustion?", a:"ΔrH = ΣΔcH(reactants) − ΣΔcH(products)."},
    {q:"Calculate ΔfH of methane given ΔcH: C −394, H₂ −286, CH₄ −890 kJ mol⁻¹.", a:"ΔfH = (−394) + 2(−286) − (−890) = −76 kJ mol⁻¹."},
    {q:"Define bond enthalpy.", a:"The energy needed to break one mole of a given type of bond in gaseous molecules (averaged over many compounds for mean bond enthalpy). Always endothermic."},
    {q:"How is ΔH calculated from bond enthalpies?", a:"ΔH = Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed)."},
    {q:"Why do bond enthalpy calculations differ from ΔH values found using Hess's law?", a:"Mean bond enthalpies are averages over many compounds; the actual bond enthalpy in a specific molecule may differ. Bond enthalpies also apply to gases only."},
    {q:"Why is bond breaking endothermic and bond making exothermic?", a:"Energy must be supplied to overcome the attraction between the bonded atoms; energy is released when new attractions form."},
    {q:"Calculate ΔH for H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl using bond enthalpies H–H 436, Cl–Cl 243, H–Cl 432 kJ mol⁻¹.", a:"Broken: 436 + 243 = 679. Formed: 2 × 432 = 864. ΔH = 679 − 864 = −185 kJ mol⁻¹."},
    {q:"Why is the enthalpy change of neutralisation of a weak acid less exothermic than that of a strong acid?", a:"Some energy is used to fully dissociate the weak acid (break the H–A bonds) before the H⁺ reacts with OH⁻."},
    {q:"Describe how to measure the enthalpy change of a displacement reaction.", a:"Measure a known volume of CuSO₄ solution in a polystyrene cup, record the temperature for a few minutes, add excess zinc powder, record the temperature at intervals, extrapolate to find ΔT, then q = mcΔT and ΔH = −q/n(CuSO₄)."},
    {q:"Why is a polystyrene cup used in calorimetry?", a:"It is a good insulator (reduces heat loss) and has a very small heat capacity, so almost all the heat goes into the solution."},
    {q:"Sketch an enthalpy level diagram for an exothermic reaction.", a:"Reactants drawn higher than products; the arrow from reactants down to products is ΔH (negative). The activation energy is the hump above the reactants."},
  ]},
  "edx_9": { title: "Kinetics I", cards: [
    {q:"Define rate of reaction.", a:"The change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time, units mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹."},
    {q:"State collision theory.", a:"For a reaction to occur, particles must collide with energy at least equal to the activation energy and with the correct orientation."},
    {q:"Define activation energy.", a:"The minimum energy that colliding particles must have for a reaction to occur."},
    {q:"Explain how concentration affects rate.", a:"More particles per unit volume, so collisions are more frequent, so more successful collisions per second."},
    {q:"Explain how pressure affects the rate of gas reactions.", a:"Higher pressure means the molecules are closer together, so collisions are more frequent."},
    {q:"Explain how surface area affects rate.", a:"A powdered solid has more surface particles exposed, so more collisions with the other reactant per second."},
    {q:"Explain how temperature affects rate.", a:"A small temperature rise greatly increases the proportion of particles with energy ≥ Ea (the main effect) and also increases collision frequency slightly."},
    {q:"Describe the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution.", a:"A graph of number of molecules against energy. Starts at the origin, rises to a peak (most probable energy), then falls with a long tail that never reaches zero. The area under the curve is the total number of molecules."},
    {q:"How does the Maxwell–Boltzmann curve change at a higher temperature?", a:"The peak moves to higher energy and is lower; the curve is broader; the area is the same; a much larger area lies beyond Ea."},
    {q:"Define a catalyst.", a:"A substance that increases the rate of reaction by providing an alternative route with a lower activation energy, and is chemically unchanged at the end."},
    {q:"How does a catalyst affect the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution?", a:"The distribution itself does not change, but Ea moves to the left so a larger proportion of molecules have energy ≥ Ea."},
    {q:"Distinguish homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts.", a:"Homogeneous: same phase as the reactants (e.g. H₂SO₄ in esterification, Cl• in ozone depletion). Heterogeneous: different phase (e.g. solid Fe in the Haber process, solid Pt in catalytic converters)."},
    {q:"How does a heterogeneous catalyst work?", a:"Reactants adsorb onto the surface (active sites), bonds weaken, the reaction occurs on the surface, products desorb."},
    {q:"Why are catalysts important industrially?", a:"Lower temperatures and pressures can be used, saving energy and money, reducing fossil fuel use and CO₂ emissions. Different catalysts can select for a particular product."},
    {q:"Give examples of catalysts used in industry.", a:"Iron in the Haber process; vanadium(V) oxide in the Contact process; nickel in hydrogenation; platinum/rhodium/palladium in catalytic converters; zeolites in cracking."},
    {q:"Draw a reaction profile for a catalysed and uncatalysed exothermic reaction.", a:"Both start and finish at the same levels (same ΔH). The catalysed route has a lower hump (lower Ea)."},
    {q:"How can the rate of a reaction producing a gas be followed?", a:"Measure the volume of gas collected (gas syringe or inverted measuring cylinder) at intervals, or the loss in mass on a balance."},
    {q:"How can the rate of a reaction involving a colour change be followed?", a:"Use a colorimeter to measure absorbance over time and convert to concentration using a calibration curve."},
    {q:"How is the initial rate found from a concentration–time graph?", a:"Draw a tangent to the curve at t = 0 and find its gradient."},
    {q:"Why does the rate of a reaction decrease over time?", a:"Reactant concentrations fall, so collisions become less frequent."},
    {q:"How are catalysts involved in the depletion of ozone?", a:"Cl• radicals from CFCs catalyse ozone breakdown: Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂; ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂. The Cl• is regenerated so one radical destroys many ozone molecules."},
    {q:"Why does a 10 °C rise roughly double the rate of many reactions?", a:"The number of molecules with energy ≥ Ea roughly doubles, even though the average energy rises only slightly."},
    {q:"What is the effect of a catalyst on the position of equilibrium?", a:"None. It speeds up forward and reverse reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster but the yield is unchanged."},
    {q:"Why is a finely divided catalyst more effective?", a:"A larger surface area provides more active sites for adsorption."},
  ]},
  "edx_10": { title: "Equilibrium I", cards: [
    {q:"Define dynamic equilibrium.", a:"In a closed system, the forward and reverse reactions occur at the same rate, so the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant."},
    {q:"What is a closed system?", a:"One in which no substances can enter or leave, so an equilibrium can be established."},
    {q:"State Le Chatelier's principle.", a:"If a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change, the position of equilibrium shifts in the direction that opposes the change."},
    {q:"How does increasing the concentration of a reactant affect equilibrium?", a:"The equilibrium shifts to the right (towards products) to use up the added reactant."},
    {q:"How does increasing pressure affect a gaseous equilibrium?", a:"Equilibrium shifts to the side with fewer moles of gas to reduce the pressure. No effect if both sides have equal moles of gas."},
    {q:"How does increasing temperature affect equilibrium?", a:"Equilibrium shifts in the endothermic direction, absorbing the extra heat. For an exothermic forward reaction the yield of products falls."},
    {q:"How does a catalyst affect equilibrium?", a:"No change in position or yield. Equilibrium is reached more quickly because both rates increase equally."},
    {q:"Write the Kc expression for N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g).", a:"Kc = [NH₃]² / ([N₂][H₂]³), units mol⁻² dm⁶."},
    {q:"Write the Kc expression for the esterification CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O.", a:"Kc = [CH₃COOC₂H₅][H₂O] / ([CH₃COOH][C₂H₅OH]), no units."},
    {q:"What does a large value of Kc indicate?", a:"The equilibrium lies to the right; the products predominate."},
    {q:"Which change alters the value of Kc?", a:"Only temperature. Changing concentration, pressure or adding a catalyst leaves Kc unchanged."},
    {q:"State the conditions used in the Haber process and explain the compromise.", a:"About 450 °C, 200 atm, iron catalyst. Lower temperature gives higher yield but too slow; higher pressure gives higher yield but is expensive and dangerous. The ammonia is removed and unreacted gases recycled."},
    {q:"Why is a high pressure favourable in the Haber process?", a:"4 moles of gas form 2 moles, so increasing pressure shifts equilibrium to the right, increasing the yield of ammonia."},
    {q:"Why is a relatively low temperature favourable for ammonia yield but not used?", a:"The forward reaction is exothermic so a low temperature gives a higher yield, but the rate would be too slow. A compromise temperature with a catalyst is used."},
    {q:"State the conditions for the Contact process.", a:"2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃: about 450 °C, 1 to 2 atm, V₂O₅ catalyst. High yield (about 99%) so high pressure is unnecessary."},
    {q:"What happens to the colour of a NO₂/N₂O₄ equilibrium when cooled?", a:"2NO₂ (brown) ⇌ N₂O₄ (colourless) is exothermic forwards, so cooling shifts right and the colour fades."},
    {q:"How does the position of equilibrium differ from the rate of reaction?", a:"Position tells you how much product forms (yield); rate tells you how quickly equilibrium is reached. A catalyst changes only rate."},
    {q:"In the equilibrium Fe³⁺ + SCN⁻ ⇌ [Fe(SCN)]²⁺, what happens when more Fe³⁺ is added?", a:"Equilibrium shifts right; the deep red colour of [Fe(SCN)]²⁺ intensifies."},
    {q:"Why is removing a product as it forms useful industrially?", a:"The equilibrium continually shifts towards the products to replace what is removed, increasing the overall yield."},
    {q:"What is the effect of adding an inert gas at constant volume to a gaseous equilibrium?", a:"No effect: the concentrations (partial pressures) of the reacting gases are unchanged."},
    {q:"What is a homogeneous equilibrium?", a:"One in which all reactants and products are in the same phase, e.g. all gases or all aqueous."},
    {q:"Why does adding water shift the esterification equilibrium to the left?", a:"Water is a product; increasing its concentration shifts equilibrium to the left to remove it, hydrolysing the ester."},
    {q:"Explain why concentrated sulfuric acid increases the yield of an ester.", a:"It acts as a catalyst and also absorbs water, a product, shifting the equilibrium to the right."},
    {q:"How does Le Chatelier's principle explain the effect of pressure on 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃?", a:"Three moles of gas become two. Increasing pressure favours the right-hand side, increasing the yield of SO₃."},
  ]},
  "edx_11": { title: "Equilibrium II", cards: [
    {q:"Define the equilibrium constant Kc.", a:"For aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD, Kc = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ), using equilibrium concentrations in mol dm⁻³."},
    {q:"Define Kp and partial pressure.", a:"Kp uses equilibrium partial pressures of gases: Kp = p(C)ᶜ p(D)ᵈ / (p(A)ᵃ p(B)ᵇ). Partial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure."},
    {q:"Define mole fraction.", a:"Moles of a gas divided by the total moles of gas in the mixture. Mole fractions sum to 1."},
    {q:"What are the units of Kp for 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ with pressures in atm?", a:"atm² / (atm² × atm) = atm⁻¹."},
    {q:"What are the units of Kc for 2HI ⇌ H₂ + I₂?", a:"No units: (mol dm⁻³)² / (mol dm⁻³)² cancels."},
    {q:"How is Kc calculated from initial amounts and the amount reacted?", a:"Set up an ICE table (initial, change, equilibrium) in moles, convert to concentrations by dividing by the volume, then substitute into the Kc expression."},
    {q:"2.0 mol of ethanoic acid and 2.0 mol of ethanol give 1.33 mol of ester at equilibrium. Calculate Kc.", a:"Equilibrium: acid 0.67, alcohol 0.67, ester 1.33, water 1.33. Kc = (1.33 × 1.33)/(0.67 × 0.67) = 3.9 (no units; volume cancels)."},
    {q:"How is Kc calculated when the volume is not given but needed?", a:"If moles of gas on each side are equal the volume cancels. Otherwise use V as a symbol and it must be given or determined."},
    {q:"How does temperature affect Kc for an exothermic reaction?", a:"Kc decreases as temperature increases because equilibrium shifts to the left (endothermic direction), lowering [products]/[reactants]."},
    {q:"How does temperature affect Kc for an endothermic reaction?", a:"Kc increases as temperature increases because equilibrium shifts to the right."},
    {q:"Explain, using Kc, why increasing a reactant concentration shifts equilibrium.", a:"Kc is fixed at that temperature. Adding reactant makes the ratio [products]/[reactants] less than Kc, so the forward reaction occurs until the ratio equals Kc again."},
    {q:"Explain, using Kp, why changing pressure does not change Kp.", a:"Kp is constant at a given temperature. Increasing total pressure changes the partial pressures; the equilibrium shifts so the ratio of partial pressures returns to Kp."},
    {q:"What is the relationship between ΔS total and K?", a:"ΔS total = R ln K. A large positive ΔS total gives a large K (reaction goes almost to completion)."},
    {q:"What does a Kc value of 1 × 10¹⁰ tell you?", a:"The equilibrium lies almost completely to the right; effectively a complete reaction."},
    {q:"What does a Kc of about 1 tell you?", a:"Significant amounts of both reactants and products are present at equilibrium."},
    {q:"How is the equilibrium concentration found when Kc and initial concentrations are known?", a:"Let x be the amount reacted, write equilibrium concentrations in terms of x, substitute into Kc and solve (often a quadratic, or a square root if the equation is symmetrical)."},
    {q:"Why is Kc not affected by a catalyst?", a:"A catalyst speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally, so the equilibrium concentrations and their ratio are unchanged."},
    {q:"In a mixture of 0.5 mol N₂, 1.5 mol H₂ and 1.0 mol NH₃ at 300 kPa, what is the partial pressure of NH₃?", a:"Total moles 3.0; mole fraction 1.0/3.0 = 0.333; p(NH₃) = 0.333 × 300 = 100 kPa."},
    {q:"Write Kp for N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g).", a:"Kp = p(NO₂)² / p(N₂O₄), units kPa (or atm)."},
    {q:"Which species are omitted from a heterogeneous equilibrium constant?", a:"Pure solids and pure liquids, because their concentration is constant. E.g. for CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), Kp = p(CO₂)."},
    {q:"How can Kc be determined experimentally for esterification?", a:"Mix known amounts of acid, alcohol and HCl catalyst, leave for a week to reach equilibrium, titrate samples with NaOH to find the remaining acid (subtracting the HCl), and calculate the other concentrations."},
    {q:"Why must the equilibrium mixture be quenched before analysis?", a:"To stop the reaction so that the concentrations do not change during the analysis, e.g. by rapid cooling or dilution."},
    {q:"Explain why the yield in the Haber process is low even though Kp is favourable at low temperature.", a:"At the compromise temperature of about 450 °C, Kp is small (equilibrium lies left). The yield per pass is about 15%, so unreacted gases are recycled."},
    {q:"State how Kc for a reverse reaction relates to Kc for the forward reaction.", a:"Kc(reverse) = 1 / Kc(forward)."},
  ]},
  "edx_12": { title: "Acid-base equilibria", cards: [
    {q:"Define a Brønsted–Lowry acid and base.", a:"Acid: a proton (H⁺) donor. Base: a proton acceptor."},
    {q:"What is a conjugate acid–base pair?", a:"Two species that differ by one proton, e.g. CH₃COOH/CH₃COO⁻ and NH₄⁺/NH₃. The acid loses H⁺ to form its conjugate base."},
    {q:"Define a strong and a weak acid.", a:"Strong: fully dissociated in water (HCl, HNO₃, H₂SO₄). Weak: only partially dissociated, an equilibrium (carboxylic acids, HF)."},
    {q:"Define pH.", a:"pH = −log₁₀[H⁺]. [H⁺] = 10⁻ᵖᴴ."},
    {q:"Calculate the pH of 0.050 mol dm⁻³ HCl.", a:"pH = −log(0.050) = 1.30."},
    {q:"Calculate the pH of 0.050 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄, assuming full dissociation.", a:"[H⁺] = 2 × 0.050 = 0.10 mol dm⁻³; pH = 1.00."},
    {q:"Define Ka and write its expression.", a:"The acid dissociation constant: Ka = [H⁺][A⁻] / [HA], units mol dm⁻³. Larger Ka = stronger acid."},
    {q:"Define pKa.", a:"pKa = −log₁₀ Ka. Smaller pKa = stronger acid. Ka = 10⁻ᵖᴷᵃ."},
    {q:"Calculate the pH of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid (Ka = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³).", a:"[H⁺] = √(Ka × c) = √(1.7 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.3 × 10⁻³; pH = 2.89."},
    {q:"State the two assumptions used to calculate the pH of a weak acid.", a:"[H⁺] = [A⁻] (dissociation of water is negligible), and [HA]eqm ≈ [HA]initial (very little dissociation)."},
    {q:"Define Kw and give its value at 298 K.", a:"Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶ at 298 K. pKw = 14."},
    {q:"Calculate the pH of 0.20 mol dm⁻³ NaOH.", a:"[H⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.20 = 5.0 × 10⁻¹⁴; pH = 13.30."},
    {q:"Why does the pH of pure water fall as temperature rises?", a:"Dissociation of water is endothermic so Kw increases with temperature, [H⁺] increases and pH falls. Water remains neutral since [H⁺] = [OH⁻]."},
    {q:"Calculate the pH after mixing 25 cm³ of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ HCl with 20 cm³ of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ NaOH.", a:"Excess H⁺ = 0.0025 − 0.0020 = 0.0005 mol in 0.045 dm³ = 0.0111 mol dm⁻³; pH = 1.95."},
    {q:"Describe the shape of a strong acid–strong base titration curve.", a:"Starts at about pH 1, rises slowly, then a steep vertical section from about pH 3 to 11 around the equivalence point (pH 7), then levels off around pH 13."},
    {q:"How does a weak acid–strong base curve differ?", a:"Starts higher (pH about 3), has a buffer region, the vertical section is shorter and in the alkaline range (pH about 7 to 11), equivalence point above 7."},
    {q:"How is a suitable indicator chosen?", a:"Its pKin (colour change range) must lie within the vertical section of the curve. Phenolphthalein (8.3 to 10) for weak acid–strong base; methyl orange (3.1 to 4.4) for strong acid–weak base."},
    {q:"Why is no indicator suitable for a weak acid–weak base titration?", a:"There is no sharp vertical section, so the colour change would be gradual and not at the equivalence point. A pH meter is used instead."},
    {q:"How can pKa be found from a titration curve?", a:"At half-neutralisation [HA] = [A⁻], so pH = pKa. Read the pH at half the equivalence volume."},
    {q:"Define a buffer solution.", a:"A solution that resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added. Made from a weak acid and its salt (acidic buffer) or a weak base and its salt (basic buffer)."},
    {q:"How does an ethanoic acid/sodium ethanoate buffer resist added acid?", a:"The added H⁺ reacts with the large reservoir of ethanoate ions: CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃COOH, so [H⁺] barely changes."},
    {q:"How does the same buffer resist added alkali?", a:"OH⁻ reacts with the ethanoic acid: CH₃COOH + OH⁻ → CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O. The equilibrium shifts to restore [H⁺]."},
    {q:"Calculate the pH of a buffer with 0.10 mol dm⁻³ CH₃COOH and 0.15 mol dm⁻³ CH₃COONa (Ka = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵).", a:"[H⁺] = Ka × [HA]/[A⁻] = 1.7 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.10/0.15 = 1.13 × 10⁻⁵; pH = 4.95."},
    {q:"How is blood pH kept constant?", a:"The carbonic acid/hydrogencarbonate buffer: H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻. Added H⁺ is removed by HCO₃⁻; added OH⁻ reacts with H₂CO₃. Blood pH stays at about 7.4."},
  ]},
  "edx_13": { title: "Energetics II", cards: [
    {q:"Define lattice energy (Edexcel).", a:"The enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid is formed from its gaseous ions. Always exothermic, e.g. Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g) → NaCl(s)."},
    {q:"Define first electron affinity.", a:"The enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous atoms each gain one electron to form one mole of gaseous 1− ions. Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g). Exothermic."},
    {q:"Why is the second electron affinity endothermic?", a:"An electron is being added to an already negative ion, so energy is needed to overcome the repulsion. O⁻(g) + e⁻ → O²⁻(g), +798 kJ mol⁻¹."},
    {q:"How is lattice energy found from a Born–Haber cycle?", a:"ΔLE = ΔfH − [ΔatH(metal) + ΔatH(non-metal) + IE(s) + EA(s)]. Work round the cycle using Hess's law, doubling terms as the formula requires."},
    {q:"Which terms are doubled in the Born–Haber cycle for MgCl₂?", a:"The atomisation and electron affinity of chlorine (two Cl atoms). Magnesium needs both first and second ionisation energies."},
    {q:"How does ionic charge and radius affect lattice energy?", a:"Lattice energy is more exothermic for smaller ions and higher charges, because the electrostatic attraction between the ions is stronger."},
    {q:"What is the difference between experimental and theoretical lattice energy?", a:"Theoretical assumes a perfectly ionic model (point charges). The experimental (Born–Haber) value is more exothermic when the compound has covalent character."},
    {q:"Explain what causes covalent character in ionic compounds.", a:"A small, highly charged cation polarises a large anion, distorting its electron cloud towards itself so electron density is shared. Polarising power: small cation, high charge. Polarisability: large anion."},
    {q:"Which Group 1 halide shows the greatest covalent character?", a:"Lithium iodide: Li⁺ is small and polarising, I⁻ is large and easily polarised."},
    {q:"Define enthalpy change of solution.", a:"The enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid dissolves in enough water to form an infinitely dilute solution."},
    {q:"Define enthalpy change of hydration.", a:"The enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous ions dissolves in water to form an infinitely dilute solution. Always exothermic, e.g. Na⁺(g) + aq → Na⁺(aq)."},
    {q:"How is enthalpy of solution related to lattice energy and hydration enthalpies?", a:"ΔsolH = −ΔLE + ΣΔhydH. The lattice must be broken (reverse of lattice energy, endothermic) and the ions hydrated (exothermic)."},
    {q:"Why is hydration enthalpy more exothermic for Mg²⁺ than for Na⁺?", a:"Mg²⁺ has a higher charge and smaller radius, so a higher charge density and a stronger attraction to the δ− oxygen of water."},
    {q:"Define entropy.", a:"A measure of the disorder of a system: the number of ways the energy and particles can be arranged. Units J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹."},
    {q:"How does entropy change on melting, boiling and dissolving?", a:"All increase entropy: solids are ordered, liquids less so, gases most disordered. Dissolving a solid usually increases entropy as the ions spread out."},
    {q:"Give the equation for ΔS system.", a:"ΔS system = ΣS(products) − ΣS(reactants)."},
    {q:"Give the equation for ΔS surroundings.", a:"ΔS surroundings = −ΔH / T, with ΔH in J mol⁻¹ and T in K. An exothermic reaction increases the entropy of the surroundings."},
    {q:"Give the equation for ΔS total and state the condition for feasibility.", a:"ΔS total = ΔS system + ΔS surroundings. A reaction is thermodynamically feasible if ΔS total is positive."},
    {q:"Explain why an endothermic reaction can be feasible.", a:"If ΔS system is large and positive (e.g. a gas forms), it can outweigh the negative ΔS surroundings, making ΔS total positive. E.g. dissolving ammonium nitrate."},
    {q:"Why do some reactions with positive ΔS total not take place?", a:"They are kinetically slow because the activation energy is very high. Thermodynamic feasibility says nothing about rate."},
    {q:"How does temperature affect feasibility?", a:"ΔS surroundings = −ΔH/T gets smaller in magnitude as T rises. Endothermic reactions with positive ΔS system become feasible above a certain temperature (where ΔS total = 0)."},
    {q:"For ΔH = +178 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS system = +161 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (CaCO₃ decomposition), find the minimum temperature for feasibility.", a:"ΔS total = 0 when T = ΔH / ΔS system = 178 000 / 161 = 1106 K."},
    {q:"Relate ΔG to ΔS total.", a:"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS system = −TΔS total. Feasible when ΔG is negative (ΔS total positive)."},
    {q:"Why does the entropy of a substance increase with temperature?", a:"Particles have more energy and move more, so there are more ways of arranging the energy among them."},
  ]},
  "edx_14": { title: "Redox II", cards: [
    {q:"Define standard electrode potential.", a:"The e.m.f. of a half-cell measured relative to the standard hydrogen electrode under standard conditions: 298 K, 100 kPa, 1 mol dm⁻³ ions."},
    {q:"Describe the standard hydrogen electrode.", a:"Hydrogen gas at 100 kPa bubbled over a platinum electrode in 1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺(aq) at 298 K. Its potential is defined as 0.00 V."},
    {q:"Why is platinum used in electrodes?", a:"It is inert (does not react) and conducts electricity. Platinum black has a large surface area and catalyses the electrode reaction."},
    {q:"What is the purpose of a salt bridge?", a:"Completes the circuit by allowing ions to move between the half-cells, keeping the solutions electrically neutral. Usually filter paper soaked in saturated KNO₃."},
    {q:"How is the standard cell potential calculated?", a:"E°cell = E°(right-hand, more positive) − E°(left-hand, more negative). Also E°cell = E°(reduction) − E°(oxidation)."},
    {q:"In a cell, at which electrode does oxidation occur?", a:"At the electrode with the more negative E° (the negative electrode, anode). Electrons flow from it through the wire to the more positive electrode."},
    {q:"E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V, E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V. Give the cell e.m.f. and overall equation.", a:"E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = 1.10 V. Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu."},
    {q:"How do E° values predict whether a reaction is feasible?", a:"The species with the more positive E° is reduced; the other is oxidised. If E°cell for the proposed reaction is positive, it is thermodynamically feasible."},
    {q:"Why might a reaction with positive E°cell not occur?", a:"Non-standard conditions (concentrations, temperature) can change E values, or the activation energy may be too high so the rate is negligible."},
    {q:"How does changing concentration affect electrode potential?", a:"Increasing [M⁺] in M⁺/M shifts the equilibrium M⁺ + e⁻ ⇌ M to the right, so E becomes more positive. Diluting makes it more negative."},
    {q:"Relate E°cell to ΔS total and K.", a:"The more positive E°cell, the more positive ΔS total and the larger K. E°cell > 0 corresponds to ΔS total > 0."},
    {q:"Describe how a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell works.", a:"H₂ is oxidised at the negative electrode (H₂ → 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ in acid), O₂ reduced at the positive electrode (O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O). Overall 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Produces a continuous voltage while fuel is supplied."},
    {q:"Give advantages and disadvantages of hydrogen fuel cells.", a:"Advantages: only water produced, no CO₂, efficient. Disadvantages: hydrogen is made using energy (often fossil fuels), storage and transport of hydrogen is difficult, expensive catalysts."},
    {q:"How do rechargeable cells work?", a:"The cell reaction is reversed by applying an external voltage, regenerating the reactants. E.g. lithium-ion cells: Li⁺ moves between graphite and LiCoO₂ electrodes."},
    {q:"Describe the method for a manganate(VII) titration.", a:"Pipette the reducing agent (e.g. Fe²⁺) into a flask with excess dilute sulfuric acid, titrate with KMnO₄ from a burette until the first permanent pink colour. Self-indicating."},
    {q:"Why is sulfuric acid used rather than HCl in manganate(VII) titrations?", a:"MnO₄⁻ would oxidise chloride ions to chlorine, giving a false titre. Nitric acid is itself an oxidising agent."},
    {q:"25.0 cm³ of Fe²⁺ solution needs 18.4 cm³ of 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ KMnO₄. Find [Fe²⁺].", a:"n(MnO₄⁻) = 3.68 × 10⁻⁴ mol; n(Fe²⁺) = 5 × 3.68 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.84 × 10⁻³ mol; [Fe²⁺] = 1.84 × 10⁻³ / 0.0250 = 0.0736 mol dm⁻³."},
    {q:"Describe an iodine–thiosulfate titration.", a:"Iodine (from oxidising an excess of I⁻) is titrated with sodium thiosulfate: I₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻. Starch is added near the end point; blue-black to colourless."},
    {q:"Why is starch added near the end rather than at the start?", a:"If added early, the starch–iodine complex is slow to break down and gives an inaccurate end point."},
    {q:"How can the concentration of an oxidising agent (e.g. Cu²⁺ or ClO⁻) be found by iodometry?", a:"React a known volume with excess KI to liberate I₂, titrate the I₂ with standard thiosulfate, then work back using the mole ratios."},
    {q:"Write the half-equation for the oxidation of Cr³⁺ to CrO₄²⁻ in alkaline conditions.", a:"Cr³⁺ + 8OH⁻ → CrO₄²⁻ + 4H₂O + 3e⁻"},
    {q:"Write the equation for the reaction of Cr₂O₇²⁻ with Fe²⁺ in acid.", a:"Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6Fe²⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O + 6Fe³⁺"},
    {q:"Why can E° values not be used directly for reactions in non-standard conditions?", a:"E values change with concentration and temperature (Nernst equation), so a reaction that is not feasible under standard conditions may become feasible, or vice versa."},
    {q:"Explain why the disproportionation of Cu⁺ is feasible using E° values.", a:"E°(Cu⁺/Cu) = +0.52 V and E°(Cu²⁺/Cu⁺) = +0.15 V. Cu⁺ is reduced (more positive) and Cu⁺ is oxidised: E°cell = 0.52 − 0.15 = +0.37 V, so 2Cu⁺ → Cu + Cu²⁺ is feasible."},
  ]},
  "edx_15": { title: "Transition metals", cards: [
    {q:"Define a transition metal.", a:"A d-block element that forms at least one stable ion with a partially filled d subshell. Sc (3d⁰ as Sc³⁺) and Zn (3d¹⁰ as Zn²⁺) are not transition metals."},
    {q:"Write the electron configurations of Cr and Cu.", a:"Cr: [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹. Cu: [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹. A half-filled or full d subshell is more stable."},
    {q:"State the characteristic properties of transition metals.", a:"Variable oxidation states, coloured ions, formation of complex ions, catalytic activity."},
    {q:"Why do transition metals show variable oxidation states?", a:"The 3d and 4s electrons are similar in energy, so different numbers of electrons can be lost without a large energy jump."},
    {q:"Define a ligand and a complex ion.", a:"Ligand: a molecule or ion with a lone pair that forms a dative (coordinate) bond to a central metal ion. Complex ion: a metal ion surrounded by ligands."},
    {q:"Define coordination number.", a:"The number of dative bonds from ligands to the central metal ion (not the number of ligands)."},
    {q:"Give examples of monodentate, bidentate and multidentate ligands.", a:"Monodentate: H₂O, NH₃, Cl⁻, CN⁻, OH⁻. Bidentate: 1,2-diaminoethane (en), ethanedioate (C₂O₄²⁻). Multidentate: EDTA⁴⁻ (hexadentate)."},
    {q:"State the shapes of complex ions with coordination numbers 6, 4 and 2.", a:"6: octahedral, e.g. [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺. 4: tetrahedral e.g. [CuCl₄]²⁻, or square planar e.g. cisplatin [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]. 2: linear, e.g. [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺."},
    {q:"Explain why transition metal complexes are coloured.", a:"Ligands split the d orbitals into two energy levels. Electrons absorb visible light of frequency matching the gap (ΔE = hν) to move from the lower to the higher level. The colour seen is the complementary colour of the light absorbed."},
    {q:"Why are Zn²⁺ and Cu⁺ compounds colourless?", a:"Both have a full 3d¹⁰ subshell, so no d–d transition is possible."},
    {q:"What factors change the colour of a complex?", a:"Oxidation state of the metal, the ligands (different splitting), and the coordination number or shape. Each alters the energy gap ΔE."},
    {q:"Describe the ligand exchange of [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ with excess ammonia.", a:"Pale blue [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ first gives a pale blue precipitate of Cu(OH)₂, then dissolves in excess NH₃: [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 4H₂O, deep blue."},
    {q:"Describe the ligand exchange of [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ with concentrated HCl.", a:"[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ → [CuCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O. Blue to yellow (green mixture). Coordination number falls from 6 to 4 because Cl⁻ is larger."},
    {q:"Why is ligand exchange with EDTA⁴⁻ favoured?", a:"One EDTA replaces six monodentate ligands, increasing the number of particles so ΔS system is large and positive. The chelate effect."},
    {q:"Describe the reactions of Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ with aqueous sodium hydroxide.", a:"Fe²⁺: green precipitate Fe(OH)₂ (turns brown at the surface in air). Fe³⁺: orange-brown precipitate Fe(OH)₃. Neither dissolves in excess."},
    {q:"Describe the reactions of Cr³⁺ with sodium hydroxide and with ammonia.", a:"NaOH: grey-green precipitate Cr(OH)₃ which dissolves in excess to give green [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻. NH₃: same precipitate, dissolves in excess (slowly) to give violet [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of Co²⁺ with ammonia and with HCl.", a:"NH₃: blue-green precipitate Co(OH)₂, dissolves in excess to form yellow-brown [Co(NH₃)₆]²⁺ which oxidises in air. HCl: pink [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ → blue [CoCl₄]²⁻."},
    {q:"Describe the colours of vanadium in its oxidation states.", a:"+5 VO₂⁺ yellow, +4 VO²⁺ blue, +3 V³⁺ green, +2 V²⁺ violet. Zinc in acid reduces vanadium(V) through all these colours."},
    {q:"Describe the interconversion of chromium species.", a:"Cr₂O₇²⁻ (orange, +6) reduced by Zn/acid to Cr³⁺ (green) then Cr²⁺ (blue). Cr³⁺ oxidised by H₂O₂ in alkali to CrO₄²⁻ (yellow). CrO₄²⁻ + acid → Cr₂O₇²⁻."},
    {q:"How do transition metals act as heterogeneous catalysts?", a:"Reactants adsorb on the surface using the partially filled d orbitals, bonds weaken, reaction occurs, products desorb. E.g. Fe in the Haber process, V₂O₅ in the Contact process."},
    {q:"How do transition metals act as homogeneous catalysts?", a:"They use their variable oxidation states to form an intermediate. E.g. Fe²⁺ catalyses S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂: Fe²⁺ is oxidised by S₂O₈²⁻ then Fe³⁺ oxidises I⁻ back to Fe²⁺."},
    {q:"Describe the autocatalysis in the oxidation of ethanedioate by manganate(VII).", a:"Initially slow, then speeds up as Mn²⁺ product catalyses the reaction: Mn²⁺ is oxidised to Mn³⁺ by MnO₄⁻, and Mn³⁺ oxidises C₂O₄²⁻ back to Mn²⁺."},
    {q:"What is cisplatin and how does it work?", a:"cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂], a square planar complex. It binds to nitrogen atoms on DNA guanine bases, preventing replication, so cancer cells cannot divide."},
    {q:"Why does the hydrated Cu²⁺ ion absorb light in the red/orange region?", a:"The splitting of the d orbitals by water ligands corresponds to red light energy. Red is absorbed, so the complementary blue colour is transmitted."},
  ]},
  "edx_16": { title: "Kinetics II", cards: [
    {q:"Define rate equation, rate constant and order.", a:"rate = k[A]ᵐ[B]ⁿ. k is the rate constant (temperature dependent). m and n are the orders with respect to A and B, found by experiment. Overall order = m + n."},
    {q:"What are the units of k for zero, first, second and third order overall?", a:"Zero: mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. First: s⁻¹. Second: mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹. Third: mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹."},
    {q:"How are orders found from initial rate data?", a:"Compare experiments where only one concentration changes. Doubling [A] with no change in rate: zero order. Rate doubles: first. Rate quadruples: second."},
    {q:"Describe the rate–concentration graphs for zero, first and second order.", a:"Zero: horizontal line. First: straight line through the origin. Second: upward curve (rate ∝ [A]²)."},
    {q:"Describe the concentration–time graphs for zero, first and second order.", a:"Zero: straight line with negative gradient. First: curve with constant half-life. Second: curve with increasing half-life."},
    {q:"Define half-life and relate it to k for a first-order reaction.", a:"The time for the concentration to fall to half its value. For first order t½ is constant and k = ln 2 / t½ = 0.693 / t½."},
    {q:"How is rate found from a concentration–time graph?", a:"Draw a tangent at the time required; rate = gradient (change in concentration / change in time)."},
    {q:"Define the rate-determining step.", a:"The slowest step in a multi-step mechanism. The rate equation includes only the species involved in this step and any steps before it."},
    {q:"How can a rate equation be used to deduce a mechanism?", a:"The orders show how many molecules of each species are involved up to and including the rate-determining step. E.g. rate = k[RBr] with no [OH⁻] means SN1 (RBr ionises slowly first)."},
    {q:"Compare SN1 and SN2 mechanisms.", a:"SN1: two steps, carbocation intermediate, rate = k[RX], typical of tertiary haloalkanes. SN2: one step, nucleophile attacks as the leaving group leaves, rate = k[RX][Nu⁻], typical of primary haloalkanes."},
    {q:"Describe the mechanism for the iodination of propanone and its rate equation.", a:"rate = k[CH₃COCH₃][H⁺]. Zero order in iodine, so iodine is not involved in the rate-determining step. The slow step is the acid-catalysed formation of the enol."},
    {q:"How does the clock reaction method find rate?", a:"Measure the time t for a fixed amount of product to form (e.g. iodine turning starch blue). Initial rate ∝ 1/t."},
    {q:"Why is 1/t a measure of initial rate in a clock reaction?", a:"A fixed small amount of product forms in time t, so the average rate over that period is (fixed amount)/t, which approximates the initial rate."},
    {q:"State the Arrhenius equation and its logarithmic form.", a:"k = Ae^(−Ea/RT). ln k = ln A − Ea/RT."},
    {q:"How is Ea determined graphically?", a:"Plot ln k against 1/T. Gradient = −Ea/R, so Ea = −gradient × R (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹). The intercept is ln A."},
    {q:"How does temperature affect k?", a:"k increases exponentially with temperature because a larger fraction of molecules have energy ≥ Ea. Roughly doubles per 10 K for many reactions."},
    {q:"What is A in the Arrhenius equation?", a:"The pre-exponential (frequency) factor: it relates to the frequency of collisions with the correct orientation."},
    {q:"How does a catalyst affect the Arrhenius equation?", a:"It lowers Ea, so e^(−Ea/RT) is larger and k increases."},
    {q:"A first-order reaction has k = 2.3 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. What is its half-life?", a:"t½ = 0.693 / 2.3 × 10⁻³ = 301 s."},
    {q:"How can the order with respect to a reagent be found using a continuous monitoring method?", a:"Plot concentration against time for one experiment. Check for constant half-lives (first order) or constant gradient (zero order), or draw tangents to find rates and plot rate against concentration."},
    {q:"Why is a large excess of one reactant sometimes used in kinetics experiments?", a:"Its concentration stays effectively constant, so the order with respect to the other reactant can be found (pseudo-first-order conditions)."},
    {q:"How does the activation energy relate to the sensitivity of rate to temperature?", a:"Reactions with a high Ea are more sensitive to temperature changes because the fraction of molecules with E ≥ Ea changes more."},
    {q:"What does a zero order reactant tell you about the mechanism?", a:"It is not involved in the rate-determining step or any step before it (it reacts in a fast step after the slow step)."},
    {q:"Hydrogen and iodine react: rate = k[H₂][I₂]. Suggest the rate-determining step.", a:"A single bimolecular step H₂ + I₂ → 2HI (or one that involves one molecule of each) is consistent with first order in each reactant."},
  ]},
  "edx_17": { title: "Organic chemistry II", cards: [
    {q:"Define a chiral centre and optical isomers.", a:"A carbon atom bonded to four different groups. Optical isomers (enantiomers) are non-superimposable mirror images that rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions."},
    {q:"What is a racemic mixture?", a:"An equimolar mixture of the two enantiomers that has no overall effect on plane-polarised light because the rotations cancel."},
    {q:"How can optical activity give evidence for an SN1 or SN2 mechanism?", a:"SN2 gives a single enantiomer (inversion), keeping optical activity. SN1 goes via a planar carbocation attacked from either side, giving a racemic mixture with no optical activity."},
    {q:"Why does the addition of HCN to a carbonyl give a racemic mixture?", a:"The planar C=O can be attacked by CN⁻ from above or below with equal probability, producing both enantiomers equally."},
    {q:"Describe the test for an aldehyde using Fehling's or Benedict's solution.", a:"Warm with the blue Cu²⁺ complex: aldehydes give a brick-red precipitate of Cu₂O. Ketones give no change."},
    {q:"Describe the test for an aldehyde using Tollens' reagent.", a:"Warm with ammoniacal silver nitrate: aldehydes give a silver mirror (Ag⁺ reduced to Ag). Ketones do not react."},
    {q:"What is the iodoform test?", a:"Methyl ketones (and ethanal, and alcohols that oxidise to them) react with iodine in alkali to give a yellow precipitate of CHI₃ with an antiseptic smell."},
    {q:"Describe the test for carbonyl compounds with 2,4-DNP.", a:"Aldehydes and ketones give an orange precipitate (2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone). Its melting point identifies the specific compound."},
    {q:"Describe the reduction of carbonyl compounds with LiAlH₄.", a:"In dry ether, aldehydes are reduced to primary alcohols and ketones to secondary alcohols. The nucleophile is H⁻. Carboxylic acids are also reduced to primary alcohols."},
    {q:"Describe the mechanism of HCN addition to a carbonyl.", a:"Nucleophilic addition. CN⁻ attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon, the C=O π bond breaks giving O⁻, which is protonated by HCN (or H⁺) to give a hydroxynitrile."},
    {q:"Why is KCN with a little acid used instead of HCN?", a:"HCN is a toxic gas and a weak acid; KCN provides a high [CN⁻] and the acid supplies H⁺ to protonate the intermediate."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of carbonyls with iodine in alkali.", a:"Methyl ketones give the yellow triiodomethane (iodoform) precipitate. Used to test for CH₃CO– groups."},
    {q:"How are carboxylic acids prepared?", a:"Oxidation of primary alcohols or aldehydes (acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, reflux); hydrolysis of nitriles with dilute acid (RCN → RCOOH + NH₄⁺)."},
    {q:"Describe the reactions of carboxylic acids with bases and carbonates.", a:"Neutralised by NaOH to form carboxylate salts; react with carbonates to give CO₂ (effervescence), a test distinguishing them from phenols."},
    {q:"How is a carboxylic acid converted to an acyl chloride?", a:"React with PCl₅: RCOOH + PCl₅ → RCOCl + POCl₃ + HCl."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of carboxylic acids with LiAlH₄.", a:"Reduced to primary alcohols in dry ether: RCOOH → RCH₂OH. NaBH₄ will not reduce carboxylic acids."},
    {q:"Describe esterification.", a:"Carboxylic acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water, heated with concentrated H₂SO₄ catalyst. Reversible, so the ester is distilled off or excess reagent used."},
    {q:"Compare acid and alkaline hydrolysis of esters.", a:"Acid (dilute H₂SO₄, reflux): reversible, gives acid + alcohol. Alkaline (NaOH, reflux): irreversible, gives the carboxylate salt + alcohol; higher yield."},
    {q:"Describe the reactions of acyl chlorides.", a:"With water: carboxylic acid + HCl. With alcohols: ester + HCl. With ammonia: primary amide + HCl. With primary amines: N-substituted amide + HCl. All vigorous, at room temperature."},
    {q:"Why are acyl chlorides so reactive?", a:"The carbonyl carbon is very δ+ because both O and Cl are electronegative and withdraw electrons, and Cl⁻ is a good leaving group."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of an acyl chloride with ammonia and name the product from ethanoyl chloride.", a:"CH₃COCl + 2NH₃ → CH₃CONH₂ + NH₄Cl. Product: ethanamide (a primary amide)."},
    {q:"Give uses of esters.", a:"Solvents, plasticisers, flavourings and perfumes; polyesters; fats and oils are esters of glycerol; biodiesel is made by transesterification."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of an alcohol with ethanoyl chloride and name the product from ethanol.", a:"CH₃COCl + C₂H₅OH → CH₃COOC₂H₅ + HCl. Product: ethyl ethanoate."},
    {q:"Explain why carboxylic acids have higher boiling points than alcohols of similar Mr.", a:"They form hydrogen-bonded dimers (two hydrogen bonds between a pair of molecules), effectively doubling the molecular mass and increasing the intermolecular forces."},
  ]},
  "edx_18": { title: "Organic chemistry III", cards: [
    {q:"Describe the bonding in benzene.", a:"Six sp² carbons in a planar hexagon with σ bonds; each carbon's remaining p electron overlaps sideways to form a delocalised π system above and below the ring. All C–C bonds 0.140 nm."},
    {q:"State three pieces of evidence for the delocalised model of benzene.", a:"All C–C bonds are the same length; the enthalpy of hydrogenation is about 150 kJ mol⁻¹ less exothermic than for three C=C; benzene does not undergo addition with bromine water."},
    {q:"Describe the nitration of benzene.", a:"Conc. HNO₃ + conc. H₂SO₄ at 50 °C. Electrophile NO₂⁺ formed by HNO₃ + 2H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + 2HSO₄⁻ + H₂O. Product nitrobenzene. Electrophilic substitution."},
    {q:"Describe the mechanism of electrophilic substitution in benzene.", a:"The electrophile is attracted to the π electrons, forms a bond to one carbon giving an intermediate with a horseshoe of partial delocalisation and a positive charge, then H⁺ is lost restoring the delocalised ring."},
    {q:"Describe the halogenation of benzene.", a:"Br₂ with a halogen carrier (FeBr₃ or AlCl₃) at room temperature gives bromobenzene. The catalyst generates Br⁺: Br₂ + FeBr₃ → Br⁺ + FeBr₄⁻."},
    {q:"Describe Friedel–Crafts alkylation and acylation.", a:"Alkylation: benzene + haloalkane with AlCl₃ gives an alkylbenzene. Acylation: benzene + acyl chloride with AlCl₃ gives a phenyl ketone (e.g. phenylethanone). Electrophiles R⁺ and RCO⁺."},
    {q:"Describe the combustion and hydrogenation of benzene.", a:"Burns with a very smoky flame (high C : H ratio). Hydrogenation: H₂, Ni catalyst, high temperature and pressure, gives cyclohexane."},
    {q:"Explain why phenol reacts with bromine water more readily than benzene.", a:"A lone pair on the O is delocalised into the ring, increasing the electron density and activating it. Bromine water is decolourised and a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol forms."},
    {q:"Describe the acidity of phenol.", a:"Weakly acidic: reacts with NaOH to give sodium phenoxide, but does not react with carbonates. The phenoxide ion is stabilised by delocalisation of the negative charge into the ring."},
    {q:"How are amines prepared?", a:"Haloalkane + excess ethanolic ammonia (heat, pressure); reduction of nitriles with LiAlH₄ or H₂/Ni; reduction of nitroarenes with Sn/conc. HCl then NaOH gives aromatic amines."},
    {q:"Explain the basicity of amines and compare ethylamine, ammonia and phenylamine.", a:"The N lone pair accepts a proton. Ethylamine > ammonia > phenylamine. Alkyl groups push electrons onto N (stronger base); in phenylamine the lone pair is delocalised into the ring (weaker base)."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of amines with acids and with acyl chlorides.", a:"With HCl: ammonium salts, e.g. CH₃NH₃⁺Cl⁻. With acyl chlorides: N-substituted amides, e.g. CH₃COCl + CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₃ + HCl."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of amines with haloalkanes.", a:"Nucleophilic substitution gives secondary, tertiary amines and finally quaternary ammonium salts, which are used as cationic surfactants."},
    {q:"Describe the reaction of amines with copper(II) ions.", a:"Amines act as ligands like ammonia: with excess butylamine, [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ forms a deep blue complex after an initial pale blue precipitate."},
    {q:"Describe the hydrolysis of amides.", a:"Acid hydrolysis gives the carboxylic acid and ammonium (or amine) salt; alkaline hydrolysis gives the carboxylate salt and ammonia (or amine)."},
    {q:"How is a Grignard reagent made?", a:"Haloalkane + magnesium in dry ether: RBr + Mg → RMgBr. Water must be excluded as it destroys the reagent."},
    {q:"What products form from Grignard reagents with carbonyls and CO₂?", a:"With methanal: primary alcohol. With other aldehydes: secondary alcohol. With ketones: tertiary alcohol. With CO₂: carboxylic acid (after dilute acid). Chain length increases."},
    {q:"Define condensation polymerisation and give two examples.", a:"Monomers join with loss of a small molecule (water or HCl). Polyesters (diol + dicarboxylic acid, e.g. Terylene) and polyamides (diamine + dicarboxylic acid, e.g. nylon-6,6; Kevlar)."},
    {q:"How do amino acids react as both acids and bases?", a:"Amphoteric: the COOH reacts with bases to form carboxylate salts; the NH₂ reacts with acids to form ammonium salts. In solution they exist as zwitterions, H₃N⁺CHRCOO⁻."},
    {q:"Describe the formation of a dipeptide.", a:"The COOH of one amino acid condenses with the NH₂ of another, losing water and forming a peptide (amide) link –CONH–."},
    {q:"Describe how amino acids can be separated and identified.", a:"Paper chromatography or TLC; the colourless spots are developed with ninhydrin (purple) and Rf values compared with known standards."},
    {q:"Outline how to plan a multi-step organic synthesis.", a:"Identify the functional group changes needed, work backwards from the target, choose reagents and conditions for each step, consider yield, safety, number of steps and whether the carbon chain length changes."},
    {q:"Give methods for increasing and decreasing carbon chain length.", a:"Increase: KCN substitution (nitrile), HCN addition to carbonyl, Grignard reagents. Decrease: iodoform reaction (methyl ketone to carboxylate with one fewer carbon)."},
    {q:"Describe how to purify an organic liquid.", a:"Separate from the aqueous layer in a separating funnel, wash with sodium carbonate solution (removes acid), dry with anhydrous CaCl₂ or MgSO₄, filter, then distil, collecting at the boiling point."},
  ]},
  "edx_19": { title: "Modern analytical techniques II", cards: [
    {q:"What does the number of peaks in a ¹³C NMR spectrum show?", a:"The number of different carbon environments in the molecule. Symmetrical carbons give one peak."},
    {q:"How many ¹³C peaks do ethanol, propan-2-ol and benzene give?", a:"Ethanol 2, propan-2-ol 2 (two equivalent CH₃), benzene 1."},
    {q:"Give typical ¹³C chemical shifts for alkane C, C–O, C=C/aromatic and C=O.", a:"Alkane 5 to 40; C–O 50 to 70; C=C and aromatic 110 to 160; C=O 160 to 220 (esters/acids 160 to 185, aldehydes/ketones 190 to 220)."},
    {q:"What does the number of peaks in a ¹H NMR spectrum show?", a:"The number of different proton (hydrogen) environments."},
    {q:"What does the area under each ¹H NMR peak (integration) show?", a:"The ratio of the numbers of protons in each environment."},
    {q:"What does splitting (spin–spin coupling) show?", a:"The number of protons on adjacent carbon atoms: a peak with n neighbouring protons splits into n + 1 lines."},
    {q:"Predict the ¹H NMR spectrum of ethyl ethanoate.", a:"Three peaks. CH₃CO singlet (3H, about δ 2.0), OCH₂ quartet (2H, about δ 4.1), CH₂CH₃ triplet (3H, about δ 1.3)."},
    {q:"Predict the ¹H NMR spectrum of propanal.", a:"CHO triplet (1H, δ 9.8), CH₂ multiplet (2H, δ 2.4), CH₃ triplet (3H, δ 1.1)."},
    {q:"Why is TMS used as a standard?", a:"Tetramethylsilane gives one sharp peak (12 equivalent H) at δ 0, upfield of most signals, is inert, volatile and miscible."},
    {q:"Why are deuterated solvents used?", a:"CDCl₃ or D₂O do not contain ¹H, so the solvent does not produce a peak in the ¹H spectrum."},
    {q:"How does a D₂O shake identify O–H and N–H protons?", a:"These protons exchange with deuterium, so their peak disappears from the spectrum when D₂O is added."},
    {q:"Why do O–H protons often appear as singlets?", a:"Rapid exchange with other molecules means they do not couple with neighbouring protons."},
    {q:"Give typical ¹H chemical shifts for alkane, CH₃CO, CH₂ next to O, aromatic, aldehyde and carboxylic acid protons.", a:"Alkane 0.7 to 1.6; CH₃C=O about 2.0 to 2.6; CH₂–O 3.3 to 4.3; aromatic 6.5 to 8; aldehyde 9.5 to 10; COOH 10 to 12."},
    {q:"How does high-resolution differ from low-resolution ¹H NMR?", a:"Low resolution shows one peak per environment. High resolution shows the splitting pattern, revealing neighbouring protons."},
    {q:"Describe HPLC.", a:"High-performance liquid chromatography: a liquid mobile phase is pumped under high pressure through a column packed with fine solid stationary phase. Components separate by retention time and are detected (e.g. UV)."},
    {q:"Describe GC.", a:"Gas chromatography: a vaporised sample is carried by an inert gas through a long column with a liquid or solid stationary phase. Components separate by volatility and solubility in the stationary phase."},
    {q:"What is retention time and how is it used?", a:"The time from injection to detection of a component. Compared with standards run under identical conditions to identify components; peak area gives the relative amount."},
    {q:"Why is GC combined with mass spectrometry?", a:"GC separates the mixture; MS identifies each component from its molecular ion and fragmentation pattern, allowing identification of unknowns in complex mixtures (e.g. forensics, drug testing)."},
    {q:"How does the stationary phase affect separation in chromatography?", a:"Components with greater attraction (adsorption or solubility) for the stationary phase move more slowly and have longer retention times."},
    {q:"What limits identification by GC alone?", a:"Different compounds can have similar retention times, and an unknown must be compared with a standard; MS is needed for definitive identification."},
    {q:"How can IR, MS and NMR together determine a structure?", a:"MS gives Mr and fragments; IR gives functional groups; ¹³C NMR gives carbon environments; ¹H NMR gives proton environments, ratios and neighbours. Combine to fit a unique structure."},
    {q:"A compound C₃H₆O₂ shows a ¹H singlet at δ 3.7 (3H) and a singlet at δ 8.1 (1H). Identify it.", a:"Methyl methanoate HCOOCH₃: OCH₃ singlet (3H, δ 3.7) and HCO singlet (1H, δ 8.1). No splitting as the groups are not on adjacent carbons."},
    {q:"Why does the CH₂ in chloroethane appear as a quartet?", a:"It has three neighbouring protons on the CH₃ group: n + 1 = 4 lines."},
    {q:"Explain why a ¹H NMR peak at δ 11 to 12 identifies a carboxylic acid.", a:"The acid O–H proton is strongly deshielded by the electron-withdrawing carbonyl group, so it appears far downfield; it also disappears on a D₂O shake."},
  ]},
};
